{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0153bTpac4BaRjaHRrNXje1r","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_7/projects/gen-depth-contamination/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"papers:\\\\n  # === Must-Cite ===\\\\n\\\\n  - id: drayson2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Machine-generated text detection prevents language model collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Drayson, Yilmaz, Lampos\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.15654\\\\\\\"\\\\n    code: \\\\\\\"https://github.com/GeorgeDrayson/model_collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"verification-curation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"direct-competitor\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Binary MGT detection + importance resampling for collapse prevention; our depth estimator generalizes their binary detector to ordinal depth\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n    methodology: >\\\\n      Binary MGT detector (encoder-only transformer + classification head; best: ModernBERT-150M, AUC .986 in-dist, .943 OOD).\\\\n      Fine-tuned 5 epochs with label smoothing α=0.1 + temperature scaling.\\\\n      Sampling Importance Resampling (SIR): w_j = (1-q(x_j))^b / Σ(1-q(x_l))^b, where q(x) = P(machine-generated), b≥1 bias term.\\\\n      Resample at k=1.5 (50% upsampling). Evaluated on GPT-2 and SmolLM2 (124M–1.7B).\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"SIR filtering not only prevents collapse but improves over human-only training (perplexity -0.85% to -25.65%, diversity +3.44% to +14.62%, MAUVE +8.46% to +20.92%)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"ModernBERT achieves best detection (AUC .986 in-dist); optimal threshold 0.8674 on imbalanced data (29% human)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Curated synthetic data has positive value — outperforms oracle (perfect filter) in equal-ratio settings\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Only evaluates fine-tuning (not pretraining), models ≤2B, open-ended generation only\\\\\\\"\\\\n    positioning_notes: >\\\\n      Closest prior work. Their SIR is a special case of our graduated filtering when K*=1 (binary: human vs all synthetic).\\\\n      We generalize binary→ordinal: estimate depth d∈{0,1,...,K*} and apply depth-graduated weights.\\\\n      K* tells when ordinal signal adds value over binary. They never discuss ordinal depth or multi-class estimation.\\\\n      Our C3 claim directly extends their framework: \\\\\\\"even coarse 3-class signal yields better calibration at decision boundaries.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: schaeffer2025position\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Position: Model Collapse Does Not Mean What You Think\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Schaeffer, Kazdan, Arulandu, Koyejo\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03150\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"model-collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"framing-critical\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Identifies 8 definitions of model collapse across 3 families; forces us to position K* as discriminability saturation (distribution deformation), not test-loss collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n    methodology: >\\\\n      Position paper. Surveys model collapse literature and identifies 8 distinct definitions organized in 3 families.\\\\n      Evaluates each definition against realistic deployment conditions. No experiments—purely analytical/argumentative.\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"8 definitions in 3 families: (A) Population risk degrading [D1: catastrophic increase, D2: any increase, D3: asymptotic divergence]; (B) Distributions deforming [D4: collapsing variance, D5: scaling law change, D6: mode disappearance/entanglement, D7: tail data loss]; (C) Spurious generation [D8: hallucinated data]\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Under realistic conditions (data accumulation, growing compute), catastrophic risk divergence (D1-D3) is avoidable\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Real harms—tail data loss (D7) and mode entanglement (D6)—have received disproportionately less attention\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Recommends researchers explicitly state which definition they study\\\\\\\"\\\\n    positioning_notes: >\\\\n      K* belongs to Family B (distributions deforming), specifically D4 (collapsing variance) + D6 (mode entanglement).\\\\n      K* measures when distribution deformation renders generations indistinguishable in feature space.\\\\n      K* is NOT about population risk (Family A). This distinction is critical for framing:\\\\n      we contribute a diagnostic for exactly the harms Schaeffer et al. identify as underexplored.\\\\n      Adopt their taxonomy explicitly in Related Work to anchor K* precisely.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: kazdan2025collapse\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Collapse or Thrive? Perils and Promises of Synthetic Data in a Self-Generating World\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Kazdan, Schaeffer, Dey, Gerstgrasser, Rafailov, Donoho, Koyejo\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16713\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"model-collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Accumulate vs replace regimes affect contamination dynamics; in accumulate regime K* may shift upward\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n    methodology: >\\\\n      Formal comparison of 3 data regimes: Replace (train on latest synthetic only; Σ→0 a.s.),\\\\n      Accumulate (train on all historical data; real fraction→0 but stability holds),\\\\n      Accumulate-subsample (fixed budget from accumulated pool; intermediate degradation).\\\\n      Theory: Gaussian setting with closed-form limits. Experiments: multivariate Gaussian, KDE, LLM SFT (Gemma2-2B on HelpSteer2/TinyStories).\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Replace: covariance collapses to 0 (a.s.), W2 diverges. Collapse inevitable within 5-10 iterations\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Accumulate: covariance stabilizes at σ₀²·sin(π/√n)/(π/√n). No collapse even as real data fraction→0\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Accumulate-subsample: degradation plateaus rather than diverges\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cardinality of real data matters more than proportion (R²=0.59 vs 0.34)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    positioning_notes: >\\\\n      Our prompt-chain = replace regime (harshest setting). K*=2 measured under replace conditions.\\\\n      This makes K*=2 a conservative lower bound: under accumulate regime (more realistic), K* would shift upward.\\\\n      Strengthens our result: if depth estimation adds value at K*=2 under replace, it adds more under accumulate.\\\\n      Cite to justify experimental design and contextualize K* as regime-dependent.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: shi2025generalization\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"A Closer Look at Model Collapse: From a Generalization-to-Memorization Perspective\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Shi, Wu, Zhang, Zhang, Tao, Qu\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16499\\\\\\\"\\\\n    code: \\\\\\\"https://shilianghe007.github.io/model-collapse/\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"model-collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Generalization-to-memorization transition in iterative retraining parallels our feature-space convergence; entropy decrease over iterations aligns with our depth-discriminative features\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n    methodology: >\\\\n      Study iterative retraining of diffusion models (DDPM) on CIFAR-10 subsets (1K–32K images).\\\\n      Generalization Score (GS): avg distance between generated and nearest training image.\\\\n      Entropy: Kozachenko-Leonenko estimator. Strong correlation: Pearson r=0.91 between entropy and log(GS).\\\\n      Mitigation: entropy-based data selection (greedy max-NN-distance + threshold-decay filter).\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"GS drops approximately exponentially across iterations; collapse within 5-10 iterations for large datasets\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Training data entropy is a direct predictor of generalization capability (r=0.91)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Entropy-based data selection mitigates memorization transition\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Diffusion models only; no LLM experiments. No theoretical analysis of WHY entropy collapses\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Generated images form tight local clusters while overall variance remains relatively stable\\\\\\\"\\\\n    positioning_notes: >\\\\n      Parallel phenomenon across modalities: their entropy decline ↔ our JSD decrease (0.27→0.004).\\\\n      Their \\\\\\\"GS drops exponentially\\\\\\\" ↔ our feature-space divergence vanishes rapidly → K*=2.\\\\n      Key distinction: they measure output-distribution entropy; we measure discriminability in feature space.\\\\n      K* complements their entropy metric: operationalizes \\\\\\\"at what depth does entropy decline = indistinguishability?\\\\\\\"\\\\n      Their clustering-while-variance-stable parallels our depth features converging before global statistics.\\\\n      Cite for cross-modal validation of convergence phenomenon.\\\\n\\\\n  # === Should-Cite ===\\\\n\\\\n  - id: basani2026diveye\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DivEye: Diversity Boosts AI-Generated Text Detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Basani, Chen\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"TMLR 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18880\\\\\\\"\\\\n    code: \\\\\\\"https://github.com/IBM/diveye\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"method-component\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Surprisal diversity features as potential baseline for depth estimation; zero-shot detector outperforms prior work by 33.2%\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: amin2025escaping\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Escaping Collapse: The Strength of Weak Data for Large Language Model Training\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Amin et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.08924\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"verification-curation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Boosting-inspired framework showing weak signal suffices for iterative training; motivates practical utility of imperfect depth estimators\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: zawalski2026codec\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"CoDeC: Detecting Data Contamination in LLMs via In-Context Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zawalski et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.27055\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"contamination\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Binary contamination detection via ICL confidence shifts; complementary to our finer-grained depth estimation approach\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: gambetta2025surplexity\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Learning by Surprise: Surplexity for Mitigating Model Collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Gambetta et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.12341\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"verification-curation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Surprise-based collapse metric; surplexity scores as potential feature for depth estimation pipeline and baseline comparison\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: zhang2024minkpp\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Min-K%++: Improved Baseline for Detecting Pre-Training Data from Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zhang, Zheng, Gao, Miao\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Binary detection baseline; does not distinguish generational depth\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: shumailov2024collapse\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Shumailov, Shumaylov, Zhao, Papernot, Anderson, Gal\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Nature 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"model-collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Seminal model collapse result; describes macro phenomenon but provides no detection tool for depth\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: alemohammad2024selfconsuming\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Self-Consuming Generative Models Go MAD\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Alemohammad, Casco-Rodriguez, Luber, Babaei, Letizia, Bastani, Baraniuk\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"model-collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Theoretical and empirical analysis of iterative generative training; complements Shumailov with formal convergence analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: dohmatob2024model\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"A Tale of Tails: Model Collapse as a Change of Scaling Laws\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Dohmatob, Feng, Yang, Charton, Kempe\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"model-collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Scaling law perspective on collapse; tail distribution loss aligns with our observed feature convergence\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: xie2023dsir\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Data Selection with Importance Resampling\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Xie, Santurkar, Ma, Liang\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"data-selection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Distribution-matching data selection baseline; does not estimate recursive depth\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: tirumala2024d4\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"D4: Improving LLM Pretraining via Document De-Duplication and Diversification\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Tirumala, Markosyan, Zettlemoyer, Aghajanyan\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"data-selection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Deduplication + diversification baseline for data curation; orthogonal to depth-based filtering\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: xie2024doremi\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DoReMi: Optimizing Data Mixtures Speeds Up Language Model Pretraining\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Xie, Pham, Dong, Du, Liu, Lu, Liang, Le, Ma, Yu\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"data-selection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Domain reweighting for pretraining; does not consider generational depth dimension\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: kirchenbauer2023watermark\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"A Watermark for Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Kirchenbauer, Geiping, Wen, Katz, Miers, Goldstein\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"watermarking\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Provenance tracking via watermarking; complementary approach to depth estimation when watermarks are available\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: zellers2019grover\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Defending Against Neural Fake News\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zellers, Holtzman, Rashkin, Bisk, Farhadi, Roesner, Choi\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2019\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Early neural text detection; binary classification paradigm that our ordinal approach generalizes\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: ippolito2020detection\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Automatic Detection of Generated Text is Easiest when Humans are Fooled\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Ippolito, Duckworth, Callison-Burch, Eck\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ACL 2020\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Binary detection difficulty analysis; does not consider multi-generational depth\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 13194, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WmnBroiyF48XyJWst59ack","session_id":"a5ad6c09-3f62-4a50-ac24-2966b7e8297b","uuid":"cdd731ac-128b-4533-b826-f4639b206168","timestamp":"2026-05-13T08:48:18.050Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01V7hYnUkKHQ4sTt5YyMSRoH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XrYHyaKRqef58Q2CWvjADc","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","input":{"project":"gen-depth-contamination","file":"paper_checklist.yaml","max_chars":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8735,"cache_read_input_tokens":19384,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8735,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":63,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WmnBroiyF48XyJWst59ack","session_id":"a5ad6c09-3f62-4a50-ac24-2966b7e8297b","uuid":"7f73de72-077d-46af-a8b3-5065794c641c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XrYHyaKRqef58Q2CWvjADc","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": false, \\\"data\\\": {}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"No paper_checklist.yaml found for 'gen-depth-contamination'.\\\", \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WmnBroiyF48XyJWst59ack","session_id":"a5ad6c09-3f62-4a50-ac24-2966b7e8297b","uuid":"e1ad896b-763b-4bc1-be04-c10deb2a3c20","timestamp":"2026-05-13T08:48:18.654Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AYq6bg6LVCocj3148FS14j","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GLo5Q27J8dFiDvVcmtVM69","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **novelty**, **related work positioning**, and **broader impact/significance**. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Generational Depth Contamination: Measuring and Exploiting the Discrimination Boundary of Recursive Synthetic Text\"\n\n**Core idea:** Web training data is contaminated by recursively regenerated synthetic text. Existing detectors are binary (human vs machine). This paper formalizes recursive generation as an iterated lossy channel, invokes DPI to prove a finite \"generational discrimination boundary\" K* — the deepest generation at which pairwise depth discrimination remains statistically reliable. They then exploit the ordinal depth signal for \"graduated filtering\" that outperforms binary filtering.\n\n**Claims:**\n- C1 (K* Characterization): K*=2 for 1.5B models under standard decoding; K*≥3 at 7B scale and T=1.2. Greedy→K*=1 creates a controllable gradient.\n- C3 (Graduated Filtering): K*-aware graduated filtering significantly outperforms binary filtering: +2.48pp MMLU, +2.70pp HellaSwag (p<0.01, 5 seeds). Gains scale monotonically with contamination rate.\n\n**Related work cited:**\n- Model Collapse: Shumailov et al. (2024), Alemohammad et al. (2024), Dohmatob et al. (2024) — characterize macroscopic collapse but don't provide depth detection tools\n- Binary Detection: DetectGPT, Binoculars, DivEye — effective at human/synthetic boundary but near-chance at adjacent depths\n- Data Curation: DSIR (distribution matching), Drayson et al. (2025) (binary importance resampling) — closest prior work, but binary\n\n**Method:**\n- Information-theoretic framework: recursive generation as iterated lossy channel, DPI guarantees finite K*\n- 15D hand-crafted features (vocab diversity, surprisal, tail distribution, structural) + LightGBM\n- Graduated filtering with 3-class ordinal signal and α-weighted gen-1\n- θ=0.60 threshold for empirical K*\n\n**Key results:**\n- K*=2 (1.5B standard), K*=1 (greedy), K*=3 (T=1.2 or 7B scale)\n- Feature degradation ordering consistent across configs (ρ≥0.878)\n- Graduated filtering: +2.48pp MMLU, +2.70pp HellaSwag vs binary (p<0.01)\n- Dose-response: gains monotonically increase with contamination rate\n- LLM zero-shot ≈ random; binary detectors plateau at depth≥1\n- 15D hand-crafted > frozen embeddings (1536D) with 85% vs 62% cross-model transfer\n- Common Crawl: correlational validation (ρ=-0.288 AlignScore, ρ=-0.342 vocab richness)\n- Retrain-chain validates prompt-chain as proxy (K* identical, AUC diff <2pp)\n\n**Models:** Qwen2.5 (1.5B instruct, 1.5B base, 7B), Pythia-1.4B. No OLMo results in paper despite being mentioned in abstract context.\n\n## Your Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty of contribution, significance, positioning vs prior work)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty concerns, missing related work, scope of impact)\n4. **Detailed Comments** — address at minimum:\n   - How novel is the K* concept? Is it a genuine conceptual contribution or a straightforward application of DPI?\n   - Is the \"iterated lossy channel\" framing novel or well-known in information theory?\n   - How does this work relate to the model collapse literature more precisely? The boundary between \"measuring depth\" and \"detecting collapse\" seems thin.\n   - Is the related work section comprehensive? Missing citations? (Consider: watermarking literature, data provenance, membership inference, iterative self-refinement, LLM-as-judge literature on quality estimation)\n   - The paper lists C1 and C3 as contributions but skips C2 — is there a missing \"degradation ordering\" contribution?\n   - Significance: is K*=2 practically useful given the coarse 3-class signal?\n   - The \"graduated filtering\" concept seems simple (just α-weighting gen-1). Is this a meaningful contribution or incremental?\n   - How does this compare to Drayson et al. (2025) more carefully? Is the improvement primarily from having 3 classes vs 2?\n   - Broader impact: does this paper open new research directions?\n   - Writing quality and presentation\n5. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n6. **Score** (1-6) with one-line justification\n7. **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":39815,"cache_read_input_tokens":22934,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":39815},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a5ad6c09-3f62-4a50-ac24-2966b7e8297b","uuid":"c07a1c07-61e6-4046-b117-6247b9096fca"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"afdff64cf4a9a2f1e","tool_use_id":"toolu_01GLo5Q27J8dFiDvVcmtVM69","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **novelty**, **related work positioning**, and **broader impact/significance**. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Generational Depth Contamination: Measuring and Exploiting the Discrimination Boundary of Recursive Synthetic Text\"\n\n**Core idea:** Web training data is contaminated by recursively regenerated synthetic text. Existing detectors are binary (human vs machine). This paper formalizes recursive generation as an iterated lossy channel, invokes DPI to prove a finite \"generational discrimination boundary\" K* — the deepest generation at which pairwise depth discrimination remains statistically reliable. They then exploit the ordinal depth signal for \"graduated filtering\" that outperforms binary filtering.\n\n**Claims:**\n- C1 (K* Characterization): K*=2 for 1.5B models under standard decoding; K*≥3 at 7B scale and T=1.2. Greedy→K*=1 creates a controllable gradient.\n- C3 (Graduated Filtering): K*-aware graduated filtering significantly outperforms binary filtering: +2.48pp MMLU, +2.70pp HellaSwag (p<0.01, 5 seeds). Gains scale monotonically with contamination rate.\n\n**Related work cited:**\n- Model Collapse: Shumailov et al. (2024), Alemohammad et al. (2024), Dohmatob et al. (2024) — characterize macroscopic collapse but don't provide depth detection tools\n- Binary Detection: DetectGPT, Binoculars, DivEye — effective at human/synthetic boundary but near-chance at adjacent depths\n- Data Curation: DSIR (distribution matching), Drayson et al. (2025) (binary importance resampling) — closest prior work, but binary\n\n**Method:**\n- Information-theoretic framework: recursive generation as iterated lossy channel, DPI guarantees finite K*\n- 15D hand-crafted features (vocab diversity, surprisal, tail distribution, structural) + LightGBM\n- Graduated filtering with 3-class ordinal signal and α-weighted gen-1\n- θ=0.60 threshold for empirical K*\n\n**Key results:**\n- K*=2 (1.5B standard), K*=1 (greedy), K*=3 (T=1.2 or 7B scale)\n- Feature degradation ordering consistent across configs (ρ≥0.878)\n- Graduated filtering: +2.48pp MMLU, +2.70pp HellaSwag vs binary (p<0.01)\n- Dose-response: gains monotonically increase with contamination rate\n- LLM zero-shot ≈ random; binary detectors plateau at depth≥1\n- 15D hand-crafted > frozen embeddings (1536D) with 85% vs 62% cross-model transfer\n- Common Crawl: correlational validation (ρ=-0.288 AlignScore, ρ=-0.342 vocab richness)\n- Retrain-chain validates prompt-chain as proxy (K* identical, AUC diff <2pp)\n\n**Models:** Qwen2.5 (1.5B instruct, 1.5B base, 7B), Pythia-1.4B. No OLMo results in paper despite being mentioned in abstract context.\n\n## Your Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty of contribution, significance, positioning vs prior work)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty concerns, missing related work, scope of impact)\n4. **Detailed Comments** — address at minimum:\n   - How novel is the K* concept? Is it a genuine conceptual contribution or a straightforward application of DPI?\n   - Is the \"iterated lossy channel\" framing novel or well-known in information theory?\n   - How does this work relate to the model collapse literature more precisely? The boundary between \"measuring depth\" and \"detecting collapse\" seems thin.\n   - Is the related work section comprehensive? Missing citations? (Consider: watermarking literature, data provenance, membership inference, iterative self-refinement, LLM-as-judge literature on quality estimation)\n   - The paper lists C1 and C3 as contributions but skips C2 — is there a missing \"degradation ordering\" contribution?\n   - Significance: is K*=2 practically useful given the coarse 3-class signal?\n   - The \"graduated filtering\" concept seems simple (just α-weighting gen-1). Is this a meaningful contribution or incremental?\n   - How does this compare to Drayson et al. (2025) more carefully? Is the improvement primarily from having 3 classes vs 2?\n   - Broader impact: does this paper open new research directions?\n   - Writing quality and presentation\n5. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n6. **Score** (1-6) with one-line justification\n7. **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n","uuid":"598e45dc-0bda-4407-bf2f-028a29557ac8","session_id":"a5ad6c09-3f62-4a50-ac24-2966b7e8297b"}
